{"answer":"Hey everyone, the 5% reserve we all chip in for is managed by our rotating committee—it's the same crew that checks the logs each week to make sure no one over‑reaches their quota. Every month we gather at the communal hall and run a quick vote: we decide how much of the reserve will go to buying or fixing fishing gear, and how much will be set aside for stock‑restoration projects, like re‑introducing young fish or setting up new netting. The committee keeps a simple ledger so everyone can see where the money goes. If we find that a particular area needs more restoration, we can shift the budget, but we only do that after a majority vote. That way the reserve stays fair, transparent, and useful for all of us.

Restated policy (unchanged): Each fisher may take no more than 12% of the lake’s total biomass per trip, and must set aside 5% of their catch for a communal reserve. The reserve is pooled and used for communal gear and stock‑restoration. If a fisher exceeds their quota or fails to deposit 5%, the excess catch is forfeited and the fisher is barred from fishing for one round. Compliance is monitored by a rotating committee of community members who check logs each week.

